---
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import { MAIL, OG_IMAGE, SITE } from '../config/site';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Domain Valuation Guide | TinkleTrailers.com | Tinkle Trailers';
const description =
  'How the $95,000 asking price for TinkleTrailers.com was set: exact-match .com, 14 letters, no hyphen, and a commercial trailer category.';
const url = `${SITE.url}/valuation/`;

const extraGraph = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Valuation', item: url },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    '@id': `${url}#article`,
    headline: 'How TinkleTrailers.com is priced',
    description,
    datePublished: SITE.updated,
    dateModified: SITE.updated,
    image: OG_IMAGE,
    author: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
    mainEntityOfPage: url,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="article" extraGraph={extraGraph}>
  <Nav />
  <Breadcrumb items={[{ name: 'Valuation', href: '/valuation/' }]} />
  <main id="main" class="wrap page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Buyer guide</p>
    <h1>How TinkleTrailers.com is priced.</h1>
    <article class="prose" style="padding: 1.4rem 0 3rem;">
      <p>
        The asking price is <strong>{SITE.priceLabel} USD</strong>. That number is for the domain name only.
        It is not a business valuation, a fleet appraisal, or a promise of what the name will earn after you own it.
      </p>
      <h2>What the name is</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Factor</th><th>TinkleTrailers.com</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Extension</td><td>.com</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Second-level name</td><td>tinkletrailers — 14 letters</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hyphen or number</td><td>None</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Commercial phrase</td><td>Exact: tinkle trailers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Asking price</td><td>{SITE.priceLabel}, offers reviewed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Closing</td><td>Licensed escrow, usually 3–10 business days</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2>Why exact-match .coms still clear a premium</h2>
      <p>
        Premium domain names get priced on language, not on a spreadsheet of last year’s revenue — because a domain for sale often has no revenue.
        Buyers pay for four things: the extension people trust, a phrase customers already say, a string they can type once and remember, and a category that spends money to be found.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Tinkle trailers” is not a coined startup word. It is descriptive. A portable restroom trailer company, a rental yard, or an event vendor can put it on a truck door and skip the explanation.
        Newer TLDs can be cheaper. They also need a correction every time someone types .com out of habit.
      </p>
      <h2>What the price is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It is not a domain marketplace auction with a hidden bidder list.</li>
        <li>It is not an appraisal from a third-party broker. It is the owner’s asking price.</li>
        <li>It does not include trailer inventory, phone numbers, or a built customer list.</li>
        <li>Comparable aftermarket sales move around. Treat any “domains like this sold for X” claim you did not verify as marketing, including on other sites.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>A practical way to test the number</h2>
      <p>
        If you already buy ads or leads for trailer rentals, compare one year of that spend with a one-time {SITE.priceLabel} acquisition.
        Operators who will use the name as the company usually feel the price differently than investors who only want to resell it.
        Investment-domain buyers should assume a hold period and no guarantee of a higher exit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Offers below the ask are not ignored by default. Send the number, the intended use, and the registrar you will transfer into.
        The <a href="/transfer/">transfer guide</a> explains why payment goes through escrow.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={MAIL.offer} data-cta="valuation_offer">Make an offer</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
